WEBVTT

00:04.662 --> 00:07.162
This is a decision story. You'll follow a miner

00:07.162 --> 00:09.750
through a fire alarm underground and make the same

00:09.750 --> 00:12.725
decisions he has to make. Pause the video at each

00:12.725 --> 00:15.275
decision and say your answer out loud before you

00:15.275 --> 00:16.325
hear ours.

00:17.436 --> 00:20.024
It's mid-shift. You're working at a face.

00:20.024 --> 00:23.549
Your W65 is on your belt, where it belongs.

00:23.549 --> 00:25.961
You walked both escapeways from this area with

00:25.961 --> 00:27.599
your instructor last week.

00:28.710 --> 00:31.035
You catch a faint smell of rotten eggs.

00:31.035 --> 00:33.760
Then you notice a strobe flashing down the heading.

00:33.760 --> 00:36.523
You don't see any smoke. The loader operator hasn't

00:36.523 --> 00:38.572
noticed anything. What do you do?

00:39.696 --> 00:42.333
At the first sign of smoke, a CO alarm,

00:42.333 --> 00:45.508
or stench gas, put on your self-rescuer right away.

00:45.508 --> 00:48.246
Don't wait to see how bad it gets. Stench gas at

00:48.246 --> 00:50.871
our mines is released into the intake fan,

00:50.871 --> 00:53.733
so it reaches every working area, and it means

00:53.733 --> 00:56.496
get out. You don't wait for smoke to prove it.

00:56.496 --> 00:58.408
Get the loader operator's attention,

00:58.408 --> 01:01.283
shut down in a safe position, and both of you start

01:01.283 --> 01:03.271
out by the primary escapeway.

01:04.386 --> 01:06.461
Notice what you didn't do. You didn't go looking

01:06.461 --> 01:08.923
for the fire. You didn't call around on the radio

01:08.923 --> 01:11.373
to ask whether it's a drill. You didn't finish

01:11.373 --> 01:14.186
the bucket you were loading. Every minute counts,

01:14.186 --> 01:16.873
because smoke and carbon monoxide travel with the

01:16.873 --> 01:19.623
air, and the air is moving toward you.

01:22.948 --> 01:25.373
A few minutes into the escapeway, the air you're

01:25.373 --> 01:29.385
breathing through the W65 is getting hot and dry.

01:29.385 --> 01:31.660
It's uncomfortable. Your partner says he's going

01:31.660 --> 01:34.398
to take his off for a second to catch his breath.

01:34.398 --> 01:35.410
What do you tell him?

01:36.526 --> 01:40.851
Keep it on. When the W65 is filtering carbon monoxide,

01:40.851 --> 01:43.301
the air it gives you gets hot and dry.

01:43.301 --> 01:46.051
That's normal. It means the unit is working.

01:46.051 --> 01:48.901
Taking it off, even for a breath or two,

01:48.901 --> 01:51.738
can expose you to carbon monoxide you can't see

01:51.738 --> 01:54.938
or smell. Keep the nose clip on, keep breathing

01:54.938 --> 01:57.576
through it, and keep moving until you reach fresh

01:57.576 --> 01:58.276
air.

01:59.392 --> 02:01.817
Tell your partner exactly that, and keep an eye

02:01.817 --> 02:04.704
on him. In smoke, people get confused and want

02:04.704 --> 02:07.917
to stop. Your job is to keep both of you moving,

02:07.917 --> 02:10.817
at a steady walk, not a run. Running burns your

02:10.817 --> 02:13.092
energy and makes you breathe harder through the

02:13.092 --> 02:16.054
unit. Stay low if the smoke is above you,

02:16.054 --> 02:18.642
keep one hand on the rib if you can't see well,

02:18.642 --> 02:21.092
and watch for the reflectors and reflective arrows

02:21.092 --> 02:23.592
that mark the way out. At our mines,

02:23.592 --> 02:26.480
white reflectors mark the primary escapeway.

02:27.660 --> 02:30.535
Further along, the smoke on the primary escapeway

02:30.535 --> 02:33.460
gets thicker. You can barely see the next direction

02:33.460 --> 02:36.035
sign, and you're starting to feel lightheaded.

02:36.035 --> 02:38.510
Do you push on, or do you change routes?

02:39.628 --> 02:42.365
Turn back and take the secondary escapeway.

02:42.365 --> 02:46.453
Here's why. The W65 filters carbon monoxide,

02:46.453 --> 02:49.340
but it makes no oxygen. If a fire has used up the

02:49.340 --> 02:53.253
oxygen in that smoke, the W65 can't help you.

02:53.253 --> 02:56.215
Feeling lightheaded in heavy smoke is a warning.

02:56.215 --> 02:58.465
The answer isn't a different unit. It's different

02:58.465 --> 03:01.516
air. Go back to the last intersection you know,

03:01.516 --> 03:04.403
follow the blue reflectors for the secondary route,

03:04.403 --> 03:06.853
and stay with your partner. This is why you walk

03:06.853 --> 03:09.516
both escapeways during training and why you pay

03:09.516 --> 03:12.428
attention when the routes change. In the dark and

03:12.428 --> 03:15.215
in smoke, you won't have time to learn the way.

03:15.215 --> 03:17.090
You'll only have time to remember it.

03:20.414 --> 03:22.864
Now suppose the secondary is blocked too,

03:22.864 --> 03:24.901
by heavy smoke you can't get through.

03:24.901 --> 03:27.276
You and your partner are in a crosscut where the

03:27.276 --> 03:29.464
air is clearer. What do you do?

03:30.584 --> 03:33.821
At Allen, escape is the plan. Our mines have no

03:33.821 --> 03:37.184
refuge chamber and keep no barricading materials,

03:37.184 --> 03:39.371
so you won't be building a barricade here.

03:39.371 --> 03:42.034
Stay together in the best air you can find,

03:42.034 --> 03:46.134
keep your W65 on, and radio your exact location.

03:46.134 --> 03:48.621
Watch for a route to clear, and follow your site's

03:48.621 --> 03:51.259
emergency plan and the people running it.

03:51.259 --> 03:53.571
Mine rescue for our sites comes from the Kentucky

03:53.571 --> 03:56.334
Division of Mine Safety's Crestwood and Hazard

03:56.334 --> 03:59.084
stations. You'll still hear about barricading in

03:59.084 --> 04:02.684
MSHA training. In general, it's a last resort when

04:02.684 --> 04:05.634
every way out is cut off. But at our mines,

04:05.634 --> 04:08.096
the site's emergency plan governs.

04:09.218 --> 04:12.318
Now take the better ending. The secondary is clear

04:12.318 --> 04:14.930
and you make it out of the portal into fresh air.

04:14.930 --> 04:17.418
When you look around, your partner isn't behind

04:17.418 --> 04:19.543
you. He was right there a minute ago.

04:19.543 --> 04:21.068
Do you go back in to find him?

04:22.196 --> 04:25.246
Don't go back in. Going back in alone is how one

04:25.246 --> 04:28.271
missing miner becomes two. Go straight to the gathering

04:28.271 --> 04:31.046
point and tell the person in charge right away:

04:31.046 --> 04:33.421
who is missing, where you last saw him,

04:33.421 --> 04:36.358
which route you were on, and when. That information

04:36.358 --> 04:38.858
is what trained rescuers need. Once you're well

04:38.858 --> 04:41.396
out in fresh air, follow the person in charge's

04:41.396 --> 04:43.421
directions about your self-rescuer,

04:43.421 --> 04:45.584
and don't go back toward the portal unless you're

04:45.584 --> 04:46.483
told to.

04:47.619 --> 04:51.544
To sum up. Stench gas, smoke, or a CO alarm means

04:51.544 --> 04:55.231
put your W65 on and get out, right away.

04:55.231 --> 04:58.506
Hot, dry air means it's working, so keep it on.

04:58.506 --> 05:02.531
The W65 makes no oxygen, so if the air is bad,

05:02.531 --> 05:05.456
change routes to fresh air. And at our mines,

05:05.456 --> 05:08.344
escape is the plan. Follow your site's emergency

05:08.344 --> 05:09.106
plan.

05:10.232 --> 05:13.394
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

05:13.394 --> 05:14.894
override this video.
